NEW CONVENTIONS IN GDL
======================

[todo: space-suppression etc., implemented for cuneify]

possible explicit grouping:

"(+"			{ gconst; GIN=0; return e_L_par_exp_p; }
"+)"			{ gconst; GIN=0; return e_R_par_exp_p; }
"(-"			{ gconst; GIN=0; return e_L_par_exp_m; }
"-)"			{ gconst; GIN=0; return e_R_par_exp_m; }


a/e₄(A) always means (a) / (e₄(A))

a:e₄(A) always means (a) : (e₄(A))

a+e₄(A) always means (a) + (e₄(A))

reimplementation: slash-colon-plus (scpgroup) stack

treat / : + as triggers to make a group containing the last grapheme
and make it the new parent

leave the group as parent until a delimiter that doesn't match the
current scp group is found

when the non-match is found, pop the scpgroup; if the new delim is an
scpgroup, create a new group that has the previous scpgroup as its
left component

SURROGATES:
===========

Surrogates are constructs such as MIN<(mu-u)>.

oxx g:surro is no longer used and surrogates are no longer implemented
as containers.  Instead, the head grapheme or group of a surrogate is
wrapped in <g:sur> and the contents of <(...)> are given status
"implied", exactly the same as when <(...)> is used for implied text
without a surrogate. Graphemes within <(...)> are also given a @g:sur
attribute which points to the ID of the g:sur grapheme/group.

In oxx multi-word surrogates were not handled correctly--the first
word was wrapped in g:surro and remaining words were not. In ax the
new approach fixes this neatly.

